1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:07,943
The brush clears a long straight sword, worn at the edges. It belongs to the last great age of the Guptas.

2
00:00:07,943 --> 00:00:16,596
Fresh invaders pressed from the north-west. Governors at home grew stronger than their king. Why do great empires fade?

3
00:00:33,817 --> 00:00:39,031
How do we know? The Bhitari pillar inscription records Skandagupta's battles.

4
00:00:39,031 --> 00:00:44,049
The Mandasor and Gwalior inscriptions record the Huna kings and the one who defeated them.

5
00:00:44,049 --> 00:00:48,038
And Gupta coins contain less gold in later reigns.

6
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:13,741
Kumaragupta ruled for about forty years, and kept the empire. After him came Skandagupta, about 455 to 467 CE.

7
00:01:13,741 --> 00:01:19,870
The Bhitari inscription says he defeated the Pushyamitras, and then the Hunas, a Central Asian people.

8
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:28,456
He also repaired the Sudarshana lake, as an inscription at Junagadh records. After Skandagupta, the empire weakened.

9
00:01:28,456 --> 00:01:34,582
The Hunas came back under Toramana and his son Mihirakula, who ruled from Sakala, now Sialkot.

10
00:01:34,582 --> 00:01:42,245
About 530 CE, the Malwa ruler Yashodharman defeated Mihirakula, according to the Mandasor inscription.

11
00:01:42,245 --> 00:01:48,910
Meanwhile, governors and feudatories, such as the Maukharis and the Later Guptas, grew independent.

12
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:59,082
The gold content of Gupta coins fell, and the state's income shrank. By about 550 CE, the empire had broken into regions.

13
00:02:14,817 --> 00:02:23,610
Scholars disagree. Why did the Gupta empire decline? One view points to outside pressure, especially the Huna invasions.

14
00:02:23,610 --> 00:02:31,477
Another view points to inner weakness: land grants that shrank the revenue, governors that grew independent, and falling trade.

15
00:02:31,477 --> 00:02:34,581
Most historians combine the two.

16
00:02:50,317 --> 00:02:56,543
Carve this tip into memory. Skanda held the Hunas;. Yashodharman broke Mihirakula.

17
00:02:56,543 --> 00:03:01,638
Two kings, two blows against the Hunas, and an empire that still fell.

18
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:33,058
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Which Gupta king fought the Hunas? The answer: Skandagupta. Number two.

19
00:03:33,058 --> 00:03:45,712
Which Malwa king defeated Mihirakula about 530 CE? The answer: Yashodharman. Number three.

20
00:03:45,712 --> 00:03:57,813
What fell in Gupta coins in later reigns? The answer: their gold content. Now one line for Mains.

21
00:03:57,813 --> 00:04:01,684
Discuss the causes of the decline of the Gupta empire.

22
00:04:21,417 --> 00:04:28,950
The sword goes into its case. It stands for the last years of a great empire. Three facts to take away.

23
00:04:28,950 --> 00:04:38,350
A long sword of the late Gupta age. Skandagupta held back the Hunas. The empire broke into regions by 550 CE.

24
00:04:38,350 --> 00:04:42,138
Next gallery: Harsha and the Pilgrim.

